\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{A finite loop comparison does not determine screening}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{On a [square torus](fdip-006M) of periods at least three, use the
[joint law](fdip-0076) with #{G=\{1,-1\}} acting by multiplication
on the real field #{\phi\in\mathbb R^V}, unit weights, #{m>0}
and #{\beta=0}.
For any simple nonempty closed edge cycle #{C}, its Wilson product
#{W_C(U)=\prod_{e\in C}U_e} has
##{\langle W_C\rangle_{\mathrm{matter}}>0,\qquad
 \langle W_C\rangle_{\mathrm{pure}}=0.}
In particular, the strict positivity in the
[three-by-three example](fdip-0078) has a direct proof independent
of rounding the determinant sums.}

\proof{
\p{Put #{D=4+m^2}. For the real scalar convention, the joint
Boltzmann factor at zero bare gauge coupling is
##{\exp\left(-\frac D2\sum_v\phi_v^2\right)
 \prod_{e\in E}\exp(U_e\phi_{s(e)}\phi_{h(e)}).}
Average the independent edge signs before integrating the matter.
Since
##{\frac12\sum_{u=\pm1}e^{ut}=\cosh t,\qquad
 \frac12\sum_{u=\pm1}u e^{ut}=\sinh t,}
the Wilson expectation is #{I_C/I_{\varnothing}}, where
##{\begin{aligned}
I_C={}&\int_{\mathbb R^V}e^{-\frac D2\sum_v\phi_v^2}
 \prod_{e\in C}\sinh(\phi_{s(e)}\phi_{h(e)})\\
 &\qquad\qquad\cdot\prod_{e\notin C}
 \cosh(\phi_{s(e)}\phi_{h(e)})\,d\phi.
\end{aligned}}
For #{C=\varnothing}, every edge contributes a hyperbolic cosine.
For nonzero vertex coordinates, the sign of the numerator's product
of hyperbolic sines is
##{\prod_{e\in C}\operatorname{sgn}(\phi_{s(e)}\phi_{h(e)})
 =\prod_{v\in C}\operatorname{sgn}(\phi_v)^2=1.}
Each cycle vertex occurs twice. All other factors are strictly positive,
so the numerator integrand is positive off coordinate hyperplanes.
Its absolute value is bounded by the denominator integrand because
#{|\sinh t|\le\cosh t}. Positive mass makes the latter integrable
by the [joint-law bound](fdip-0076). Both integrals are therefore
finite and strictly positive. In the pure law, independent uniform
edge signs give zero expectation for any nonempty product of distinct
edges, proving the comparison.}
}

\p{Integrating matter has changed the connection law, so the exact
pure-gauge formula cannot be reused unchanged. On tori with periods
at least 6, the [closed-walk calculation](fdip-0072) gives a separate
controlled large-mass explanation: matter generates a positive
plaquette coefficient and higher loop terms. Its order #{D^{-6}}
remainder is not being applied to the three-by-three example, where
short winding cycles occur.}

\p{Neither the finite comparison nor the positive plaquette term
establishes an asymptotic area law, a perimeter law or string breaking.
Such a claim needs a specified family of volumes and loops, a limiting
regime and control of the higher terms. Gliozzi and Rago study a
different system: a three-dimensional two-element gauge group coupled
to Ising matter taking only the values #{\pm1}, rather than an
unbounded Gaussian field. Their Section 3, equation (12), fixes that
action; their analysis of Wilson-loop overlap with a broken-string
state is model-specific context \citet{pp. 2, 5}{gliozzi2004overlap}.
It does not supply a screening theorem for the present Gaussian law.}
